---
// Figure: one request through Redis 8 with I/O threads enabled (stepped).
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import './redis.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="redis-io-threads" title="A GET with I/O threads enabled" controls="steps"
  legend={[{ kind: 'client', label: 'Request' }, { kind: 'redis', label: 'Main thread' }, { kind: 'hit', label: 'Reply' }]}
  caption="The order of work in the Redis 8.0 I/O threads implementation (src/iothread.c in Redis 8.10.3). Simplified: the hand-offs between threads go through queues, an I/O thread serves many clients at once, and some connections (Pub/Sub, blocking commands, client-side caching) always stay on the main thread.">
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Three lanes: the client, the I/O thread that owns the client's connection, and Redis's main thread, on a server running io-threads 3 (the main thread and two I/O threads). The client sends GET. The I/O thread reads the bytes from the socket and parses them into a command, then hands the parsed command to the main thread. The main thread executes GET against the keyspace and prepares the reply, then hands the client back. The I/O thread writes the reply to the socket and the client receives it.</p>
    <p>The socket work moved to another thread; executing the command did not. A command that takes 100 ms on the main thread still makes every client wait 100 ms, with or without I/O threads.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { sequenceFigure } from '../shared/sequence.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="redis-io-threads"]').forEach(root => {
    sequenceFigure(root, {
      label: 'A GET request passing between a client, an I/O thread and the Redis main thread',
      intro: 'Redis runs with io-threads 3 on a 4-core machine: the main thread plus two I/O threads, leaving a core spare. This client’s connection has been assigned to one of the I/O threads.',
      lanes: [
        { id: 'client', label: 'Client', kind: 'client' },
        { id: 'io', label: 'I/O thread', kind: 'neutral' },
        { id: 'main', label: 'Main thread', kind: 'redis' },
      ],
      initial: { client: 'waiting', io: 'idle', main: 'other clients' },
      steps: [
        { from: 'client', to: 'io', label: 'GET k', kind: 'client', state: { io: 'read, parse' }, explanation: 'The client sends GET. The bytes arrive on a socket that belongs to an I/O thread, which reads them and parses them into a command. Without I/O threads, the main thread would do this itself.' },
        { from: 'io', to: 'main', label: 'command', kind: 'client', state: { io: 'idle' }, explanation: 'The I/O thread hands the parsed command to the main thread and goes back to its other clients.' },
        { at: 'main', note: 'execute GET', state: { main: 'executing' }, explanation: 'The main thread executes GET against the keyspace and builds the reply. Only the main thread ever touches the data, so commands still run one at a time, and each is still atomic.' },
        { from: 'main', to: 'io', label: 'reply', kind: 'hit', state: { main: 'other clients', io: 'write' }, explanation: 'The main thread hands the client back to its I/O thread and moves on to the next command. The I/O thread writes the reply to the socket.' },
        { from: 'io', to: 'client', label: 'value', kind: 'hit', state: { io: 'idle', client: 'done' }, explanation: 'The client receives the value. The socket work ran on another thread; the command did not. A slow command still blocks every client.' },
      ],
    });
  });
</script>
